{ Playing this will give you many enemies in chess. It is hated because it’s such a solid opening and black can’t really counter it. Let’s Push d4. }
1. d4 d5 { Now we play the move that distinguishes the London. Bf4. } 2. Bf4 Nf6 { We should solidify our position by playing pawn to e3, which defends our d pawn from aggressive attacks. } 3. e3 c5 { Now we play what is known at the London wedge. it’s hard to break through, so let’s solidify our d pawn by protecting it even more with our c pawn. } 4. c3 { [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Ge3,Gf2,Gb2] } (4. Nf3 { Pushing our knight to f3 is wrong as even though solidifies our d pawn, it allows for aggressive counter play to the London, which we don’t want. }) (4. Bb5+ { This is wrong as now they have agressive play, which we don’t want. }) 4... Nc6 5. Nd2 Qb6 { This move goes for our pawn immediately, so we defend the pawn with Qb3 (obviously). } 6. Qb3 Qxb3 { This move is the most common, trading queens. } { [%cal Ga2b3,Gd2b3] } 7. axb3 { Now we have a very strong attacking position. c4 is coming and they can threaten us very little. } { [%csl Rc4,Gf8][%cal Ga1a6,Rc3c4,Gg1f3,Gf1e2,Ge1g1,Gh1f1] } (7. Nxb3 { This move recaptures also but they can lock down their position and get aggressive play by pushing their pawn to c4. } 7... c4 8. Nd2 b5 9. Ngf3) *


