{ Let’s push e3 }
3. e3 Bf5 { Sometimes they move symetrically. We just push c3 as regular. } 4. c3 e6 { Simply develop our knight to f3 } { [%csl Gg1] } 5. Nf3! { [%cal Gf3h4,Gh4f5] } 5... c6 { They also create the wedge, and so let’s attack their bishop. } { [%csl Gc6,Gd5,Ge6,Gf7,Gb7] } 6. Nh4! Be4!? { Attack their bishop again. } 7. Nd2 { [%cal Gd2e4] } (7. f3 { Also works but the computer says it is -0.9 advantage. We want to stay accurate. Apparently there is } 7... Bxb1 8. Rxb1 Nh5 9. Bxb8 Qxh4+ 10. g3 Qd8 11. g4 (11. Bf4 g5 { [%cal Gg5f4] } 12. Be5 f6 { [%cal Gf6e5] } 13. g4 fxe5 14. gxh5) 11... Qh4+ 12. Kd2 Rxb8 13. gxh5 Qxh5) 7... Qb6 { Now we attack again with the pawn } { [%csl Gf2] } 8. f3 { This does weaken our kingside, but it's ok since they don't have their queen pointing at it. Plus, we can castle very soon. } 8... Bg6 { Let’s trade. } { [%cal Gh4g6] } 9. Nxg6 hxg6 { Let’s defend aggressively. } { [%cal Gd1b3] } 10. Qb3 Qxb3 { [%cal Ga2b3] } 11. axb3 { This is a good position. } { [%cal Ga1a6,Gc3c4,Ge3e4,Gf1e2,Ge1g1,Gh1f1] } *


