3... g6 { This time they play 3. g6. We should work on creating our wedge. } 4. e3 Bg7 { This was obvious. Our solidity is enough that we can neglect to develop pieces for now. Let’s make the wedge again. } 5. c3 { [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Ge3,Gf2,Gb2] } 5... O-O { Develop our knight to d2. } 6. Nd2 { They have 3 different moves that are logical. Bf5, c5, and Nc6. } { [%cal Gc7c5,Gb8c6,Gc8f5] } 6... c5 { In this case they play c5. Let’s develop our knight to f3. } { [%csl Gg1] } 7. Ngf3 { (Note: Qb6 is easily met with Qb3). } 7... Nc6 { They develop logically. Let’s play be2. (You could also do Nb1, undeveloping your knight completely. Then something could happen like Bf5 c4 dxc4 Bxc4 cxd4 exd4, however I don’t recommend this. } 8. Be2 Nh5 { They attack our bishop, let’s put it on g5 } 9. Bg5 h6 { Retreat again to h4 } 10. Bh4 $44 g5!? { Retreat. } 11. Bg3 { This look bad for us, but they have cleared a long diagonal near their king that our bishop will poke holes in, causing many problems for black. For Example. } { [%cal Gb1h7] } 11... f5 { [%cal Gg3e5] } 12. Be5 g4 { [%cal Ge5g7] } 13. Bxg7 Kxg7 { [%cal Gf3e5] } 14. Ne5 Nxe5 { [%cal Gd4e5] } 15. dxe5 d4 { [%cal Gc3d4] } 16. cxd4 { [%cal Gc5d4] } 16... cxd4 { [%cal Gd2b3] } 17. Nb3 dxe3 18. fxe3 Qxd1+ { [%cal Ga1d1] } 19. Rxd1 { We’ve got an open file and their king’s out in the open. The best move is literally Kg6 for black. } { [%cal Ge1h1,Gh1f1,Gd1d8] } 19... Kg6 *


