6... Bf5 { This time they play Bf5. Let’s develop our knight to f3 } { [%cal Gc8f5] } 7. Ngf3 Nc6 { Let’s move our bishop to the best square, setting up castle and attacking positions. } { [%csl Gf1] } 8. Bb5 Nh5 { Let’s move our bishop to g5. } 9. Bg5 h6 { This may look bad for us, but we simply retreat to h4. } 10. Bh4 { You might think that there is g5 but this is a blunder. This repertoire is not supposed to be about blunders, since they usually aren’t played, but this is a common one I wanted to point out. } 10... g5?? { Take the pawn. } { [%csl Gf3] } 11. Nxg5!! hxg5 $16 { Now we take their hanging knight. } { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gd1h5] } 12. Qxh5! { We’re attacking the g pawn, and the top computer move is actually f6. } { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gh5g5,Gh4g5] } 12... f6 { Counterattack with g4 } { [%csl Gg2] } 13. g4 Bh7 { Now we have a strong attacking position, and the computer rates us up +2.5.Just don't forget to retreat you bishop to g3! (its hanging) } 14. Bg3 $18 { Nice! } *


