{ Another way you can play instead is defending with our e pawn, setting up the wedge formation again }
6. e3 cxd4 { They took, because, well, they want space. } 7. exd4 Nc6 { Defend with the c pawn. } 8. c3 Bf5 { Develop your g knight to a more active square. } { [%csl Gg1] } 9. Nf3 e6 { Let’s move our c bishop to a more active square. } { [%csl Gc1] } 10. Bf4 (10. Bg5 { This doesn’t work since they can get aggressive with us and play pawn f6 } 10... f6 { and there’s stuff like } 11. Be3 e5) 10... Nf6 { Let’s develop our d knight, partially setting up the position in the chapter at the start. } 11. Nbd2 Bd6 { Trade } 12. Bxd6 Qxd6 { Let’s get aggressive, Bb5. } 13. Bb5 O-O { Here it transitions from the opening to the middle game. This is all the theory you need to know. } { [%cal Gd1b3,Gb3b7,Gb5e2,Ge1h1,Gh1f1] } *


[Event "The London System (Lifetime Repertoire): More in depth on 2. e3"]
[Date "2025.12.09"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System, Steinitz Countergambit"]
[StudyName "The London System (Lifetime Repertoire)"]
[ChapterName "More in depth on 2. e3"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/artuehhC/35tiZHPS"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Carrul"]
[UTCDate "2025.12.09"]
[UTCTime "22:12:37"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Let’s Play the London! }
1. d4 d5 { Let’s play the London! } 2. Bf4 c5 { Let’s play the London! } 3. e3 Nc6 { Obviously we make the wedge. } 4. c3 { [%csl Gc3,Gb2,Gd4,Ge3,Gf2] } 4... Qb6 { calmly defend with Qc2 } 5. Qc2 Nf6 { Develop your g knight according to the golden moves. } { [%csl Gg1] } 6. Nf3 g6 { Develop your other knight. } 7. Nbd2 Bg7 { In this variation you have 2 choices, bd3 or be2. Bd3 loses tempo, so I will show Be2. } { [%cal Gf1d3,Gf1e2] } 8. Be2 { (Note: Nh5 is easily met with bBe5, destroying their fianchetto.) In this case, they play 0-0. } 8... O-O { Copy black. } 9. O-O Nh5 { Destroy their fianchetto. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gf4e5] } 10. Be5 Nxe5 { [%csl Gf3,Ge5] } 11. Nxe5 f6?? { Here they play a nonchalant attacking move, but this is a blunder. Their knight has no safe squares to go to, and soon we will capture it. } { [%csl Gh5,Gd4][%cal Gd4c5] } 12. dxc5 Qxc5 { [%csl Ge5,Gd3] } 13. Nd3 { [%cal Gd3c5] } 13... Qc7 { And now we play the crazy move,... g4! } 14. g4! { This looks bad since our kings safety is being exposed, but all our pieaces are protecting places around our king. We will get the knight on the next move. } 14... e5 { Take. } 15. gxh5 gxh5 { We need all the material advantage we can get. :) } { [%csl Ge2] } 16. Bxh5 { They’re completely losing. All you need to do is mitigate their pawn storm in the center and you should definitely win. } { [%csl Gd5,Ge5,Gf6][%cal Gd5d1,Ge5e1,Gf6f1] } *


