{ Here they Play Nh5, attacking the bishop. } { [%cal Gd4c5,Gf4e5,Ge5d4,Gf6h5] }
6... Nh5 { We must trade with their c pawn so we can get a square for our bishop to move to. } 7. dxc5 Qxc5 { Obviously they took back. } 8. Be5 f6 { Here they played pawn f6, attacking our bishop, move it to the powerful d4 square. } { [%cal Gf6e5] } 9. Bd4 Nxd4 { They took, and now we take back with our pawn. } 10. exd4 (10. cxd4 { This doesn’t work since hang our queen. }) 10... Qc6 { We have successfully stopped the knights attack. :) } *


[Event "The London System (Lifetime Repertoire): London Line #3"]
[Date "2025.12.14"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "The London System (Lifetime Repertoire)"]
[ChapterName "London Line #3"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/artuehhC/JahO5SNe"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Carrul"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3p4/3P1B2/8/PPP1PPPP/RN1QKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2025.12.14"]
[UTCTime "19:13:32"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ They play Nc6. }
2... Nc6 { Honestly, I have 0 idea why 24% play this. They silence their own attack of ...c5 and ...Qb6 and are ultimately are really passive. Maybe their idea is something like ...f6 ... e5. Honestly, I don’t know. They’re handing us a good game with rarely any threats. c3 } 3. c3 { This gives our queen more space and we are preparing ...Qb6 if their c bishop moves. } 3... Nf6?! { Again, so passive! They block their own threat of f6 themselves, usually played 36% of the time! First things first, the wedge should be made. We have the threat ...c4 looming, and this is very pleasant for us. } 4. e3 Bf5 { Another common move, played 36% of the time. WE should trade with their powerful bishop, since they’re taking us out of our comfort zone. Bd3. } { [%csl Gf1] } 5. Bd3 Bxd3 { Recapture, of course. } 6. Qxd3 e6 { That’s played 76% of the TIME!!!! We’ve got ideas of ...Qb5, but it's too early for aggressive play, not when you're not finished developing. The order doesn’t really matter right now, but stockfish prefers Nf3, leaving our queen more space to retreat of attacked, also threatening ...Ne5. } { [%cal Rd3b5,Rb5e8,Rb5b7] } 7. Nf3 (7. Qb5) 7... Bd6 { Usually we don’t want to trade since Bxd6 Qxd6 get’s their queen into the game and allows them to connect rooks on the next move. This also leaves the option of O-O-O for black, setting up a pawn storm they can attack our king with. It’s better to avoid all these problems and retreat our bishop to g3 } 8. Bg3 Bxg3 { You have the choice of hxg3 or fxg3. The computer likes hxg3, but that already creates a very clear passed pawn for black. I prefer fxg3. However, since fxg3 is -0.5 and hxg3 is +0.3, I cannot blame you if you play hxg3. I also like fxg3 since it opens up the f file for the rook when you play ...O-O. } 9. fxg3 O-O { Nd2 finishes our development and there is no problem with it. } 10. Nbd2 $13 { This is good for us. (Did you know that playing 10. Ne5 wins 71% of the time for white?!) } { [%cal Rd3b5,Rb5b7] } *


